@node Timely Motivation
@section Motivation

Timely dataflow is a system for implementing data-parallel streaming
computation, and, viewed from the right angle, a way to structure
computation generally.  It arose from work on scalable, distributed data
processing platforms whose goal was to provide both @emph{expressive
computation} and @emph{high performance} at once: sophisticated
streaming computations, including iterative control flow, that
nonetheless execute with only a small amount of overhead and
synchronization.

A timely dataflow program is a graph of operators connected by streams.
Three properties make the model worth adopting:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Data parallelism.}  Operators are largely data-parallel: they
operate on independent parts of the data concurrently.  The runtime
distributes a computation across parallel workers, whether threads on
one machine or threads across a cluster, which raises throughput and
lets a program scale to larger problems.
@item
@strong{Streaming data.}  The core data type is a @emph{stream}: an
unbounded sequence of data that arrives as the computation proceeds, not
all at once.  A streaming program explains both how to respond to a
static input (feed it all in at once) and how to react to data that
arrives later.
@item
@strong{Expressivity.}  Timely dataflow adds higher-level control
constructs, chiefly iteration, over a traditional stream processor.
This moves a stream computation from straight-line code to the world of
algorithms.
@end itemize

@subsection When to use timely dataflow

Reach for timely dataflow when streaming and scalability matter: a
scalable monitoring application, a big-data computation over more data
than fits in memory, or an incremental iterative computation over a
large graph or matrix.  Even a program as simple as @command{grep}, when
stated as a dataflow computation, scales out to multiple threads and
across machines for free.

@subsection When not to use timely dataflow

Timely dataflow is a @emph{dataflow} system: at its core it moves data
around.  That makes it a poor fit for problems that would rather not
move data, such as sorting a single in-memory slice, where a data-
parallel library like a work-stealing thread pool suits better.  It is
also a poor fit for algorithms that are correct only because one step
happens strictly before another, such as depth-first search, unless you
are willing to re-imagine the algorithm as the movement of data.
Finally, timely dataflow sits between a language library and a runtime
system, so it offers neither the stability of a plain library call nor
the surrounding infrastructure of a managed cluster framework; some of
that burden passes to you.
